tic uh  implications and you can actually  integrate that into this flow scheme  where you can  include the ck13 staining into your  decision entry as to whether these  patients should be followed up or you  could dei intensify the follow-up  something very similar can also be done  in the context of vulva intraepithelial  neoplasia where also you can combine  these co 13 stains or ck17 stains with  for instance proliferation markers such  as mib1  providing additional information  something that as a thoracic pathologist  has my particular interest is tissue  preservation in the context of small  biopsies or cytology of lung cancer  because nowadays we have to do quite a  few tests to actually provide a  predictive profile for these tumors so  if you are dealing with a squamous  carcinoma  you can sometimes get away with just  doing a pdl-1 stain to see whether these  patients actually qualify for immune  oncology or whether they are treated by  chemotherapy alone  sometimes  in never or light smokers or younger  patients you may also want to add  additional molecular uh profiling which  is routine in fact in the non-squamous  cell carcinoma so  there are there's an increasing list of  different predictive markers that we  need to  ascertain in these often very small  biopsies and that's based for instance  in the case of pdl1 now on quite a large  a number of different clinical studies  which have proven the efficacy of pdl1  as a predictive marker for immunotherapy  so the tissue preservation protocol  prescribes that you try to do as little  as possible prior to doing all this  predictive testing  and that would  also include the use of immunochemical  markers which you need to identify the  different subtypes of non  non small cell carcinoma that we know in  clinical practice so the  squamous cell carcinoma versus the non  squamous mainly  and routinely we would do that by a  single humanistic chemical stains  however that's also possible to combine  uh in a multiplexing technique for  instance in this case we have a  psychological preparation here on the  left in the h e stain on the right you  can see that ttf1  stains the nuclei and then napsin  stains the cytoplasm of this  adenocarcinoma of the lung so that's  something that we routinely use  admittedly these are of course only two  markers at the  moment  you can also do that in small biopsies  with very much the same results so here  you can confirm that's an adenocarcinoma  up along without having to do two  separate stains you can add in a  different stain to that  to see whether you can actually also  exclude  any squamous cell  differentiation  so something something else that's  interesting to us when it comes to using  multiplexing in the routine setting  would be layered analysis so in this  case for instance we're interested in  the proliferation rate of the epithelium  in this slide and not so much the  proliferation which is happening outside  of the epithelium so here you can see uh  in red the uh pan keratin stain whereas  in  the brown you can see the ki-67 stain  so that would be much easier to actually  interpret and do the counting of these  stains something that we've also used in  layered analysis  is  the analysis of a  a lymph node in this very topical case  of a covid19 patient who by using the h  and e was  suspicious of having had a  lymphohystocytosis and here we used a  multiplexing technique where we used a  cd68  and  in this case there's cd3 stain in the  middle uh at the bottom and a uh an mpo  stain in blue  at the right uh bottom  picture here where we can see that you  can see inclusion of both t cells  and granulocytes in these macrophages  and thereby proving that in fact we're  dealing with this hla  type  disorder in covent 19 in this case  going back to immuno oncology you can  also appreciate here by just using the  pdl1 stain in non-small cell lung cancer  on the on the left which is staining the  the epithelial cells but in a in a  different patient the epithelial cells  are actually negative but the  macrophages are positive and by  morphology alone particularly  to somebody who's not used to looking at  uh non-small cell lung cancer sections  this may be very difficult to  distinguish  so it would be good if we could actually  combine pdl-1 staining with for instance  here on the right in pink the ttf1 stain  and also in yellow  the pan keratin stain so here we can see  by using this multiplex approach with  these three different stains of pdl-1  and  also the keratin and ttf1 but this is  tumor  and this in fact  is an area of immune cell filtration  which by using pdl-1 alone is very  difficult to distinguish  so this is a potential application which  i think we'll see more and more of in  the years to come  of course it would be ideal to also  combine  the pan keratin stain with a pdl-1 and  macrophage stain in one single  multiplex method and that's been shown  here  so you can actually determine which  cells  are tumor cells and which cells are  macrophages in just a single slide and  that is also something that can then be  used for automated uh digital analysis  so in this particular biopsy you can see  that there's just a small glandular  structure which may be only present in  one particular section so if you do  another section that may be actually cut  out of this tissue so it would be very  useful to actually do this particular  multiplex stain on that single section  to determine that this is in fact an  invasive  glandular structure as opposed to the  ones which are  close to it which have this intact basal  epithelial layer  you could also combine different stains  to do cellular sub phenotyping in situ  in  slides where here for instance  these cells were stained with cd3 which  of course denotes t lymphocytes but also  with cd8 which points towards a  cytotoxic phenotype and cd4 which is a t  helper cell phenotype and then combining  those you can actually define these  different populations by combining the  different stains  now this is a case where we use this  type of cellulose of phenotyping so this  is a 58 year old male with  lymphadenopathy of unknown origin  and who on a previous ebis diagnosis was  shown to have atypical cells which were  suggestive for non-small cell lung  carcinoma  then  the clinicians apparently were unsure as  to how true to treat those patients and  luckily they decided to do a  mediocenoscopy and removed lymph nodes  for analysis that showed  the following picture in the h e stain  where we can see that there's an  extensive infiltrate of  small lymphocytes and there's quite a  lot of sclerosis there's the odd used in  the fill  and if you see this sort of picture  particularly if there are some larger  cells in this  infiltrate you might suggest that this  could potentially be a hodgkin lymphoma  but of course that needs to be confirmed  then a cd20 stain was done showing an  infiltrate infiltrator b lymphocytes and  perhaps a few larger cells cd30 didn't  show that much in the way of staining  and the cells were not obviously  atypical and not enlarged  cd15 did show a few larger cells with  also a paranuclear dot that's already  suspicious and then pax 5 which might  also stain  hodgkin's cells was also positive in  some of the larger cells but was not  particularly convincing  interestingly there was also positivity  for ebv so we couldn't reach a certain  diagnosis and then  we decided to use a  multiplex immunostain  including cd15 cd20 and cd30 where cd20  is showing all of these pink cells and  then  cd15 that's the yellow yellow stain and  cd30 is the green stain surrounding them  you can actually see that cd30 and cd15  co-localize very nicely in these cells  which were then  shown to be hodgkin's cells whereas the  surrounding cd20 positive cells  are reactive b cells so we can actually  make a confidence diagnosis of a  classical non-hodgkin lymphoma in a  minutes amount of material 