ct  behavior of particular tumor to  immunotherapy in non-small cell lung  cancer increasingly it's also known that  intra-tumor  t-cells may play a role  so if you haven't got any t-cells the  tumors unlikely to respond maybe you  might also need to look at  tumor sensitivity or immune effects such  as ms mhc expression  and also potentially to look at  mutational load as a surrogate markup  for tumor foreigners and we've  combined  these approaches in some of the studies  that we've done and this is a different  paper in which uh in fact these authors  looked at a meta-analysis of these  various techniques  as a standalone and it was seen uh that  by using uh the multiplex  immunochemistry  uh approach you can actually achieve a  very good area under the curve with a  very good uh positive and negative  predictive value so this actually uh  outperforms some of the uh the more  classical markers that we know which are  predictive for  response to immunotherapy and by  combining all of these you can actually  achieve a very good  pool specificity  so you can do that by looking at all  these  components in isolation so for instance  looking at exclusion of cd8 cells again  by using multiplex techniques  and we've also done this  in  small biopsy specimens and a  retrospective study  in which we looked at various parameters  in isolation  so we found that patients who had a very  low pdl1 low cd8 infiltration low hla  expression  and  also a high html  in this case  do quite badly whereas if you have a  high tml  and a high pdl1 expression high cd8  expression and presence of hla  you tend to respond better to  immunotherapy  and that is borne out  by  doing the separate analysis for pdl1  both for uh progression-free survive on  overall survival  cd8 is not so predictive an hla loss by  itself is also not predictive  but if you combine all of those and you  do unsupervised clustering you can  actually see  that by  combining all of these positive  phenotypes you can 