oned so this is uh the uh  classifier analysis where you can  actually distinguish different areas of  the tumor you can do this nearest  neighbor analysis or to look for  differences or to look for distances  between different immune cells and a  tumor for instance which is also done  here and you can look for infiltration  of the tumor by immune cells by doing  this type of deep learning approach and  then hopefully eventually you could  combine all of those approaches and then  come up with a predictive model  to classify this tumor with  now to finish off  i think it's important that all of these  steps require of course uh rigid  validation  so  the pre-processing phase is important  you know how long do you keep the tissue  on full line for for instance uh then  the process itself its  stage itself is important of course to  validate the staining procedure  the analytical phase  and then eventually also  the ai  algorithms which are trained using all  of these images so all of these need to  be validated uh in your own uh  laboratory uh both on that from a  technical point of view uh internally  but also potentially also eventually  externally uh you also have to validate  it against the singleplex staining as i  mentioned earlier so you need to be sure  that what you're looking at in multiplex  is actually a true representation of the  multiple single flex staining  you also have to perhaps think about  validating it against the pathologist  assessment so if you're using these ai  approaches  you need to be sure that if you're going  to use that in a diagnostic setting it's  at least as good as a pathologist in  doing the same sort of assessment  then you have to make sure if you  do that type of  analysis that the staining consistency  is right that you  have a good scanner with the right  magnification which is consistent as  well you have to choose the right  analytical software which it can produce  with consistent results  the settings are important how 